course: biology
lesson: 293
title: Horses, cattle, camels and chickens: how people tamed animals
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: When you ride a pony you are sitting on thousands of years of history. Wild horses once ran free on the grasslands of what are now Ukraine, Russia and Kazakhstan, and people there learned to keep and ride them.
explain: Taming is not the same as training. Training teaches one animal tricks in its lifetime; taming, or domestication, means people choose the calmest animals to have babies, generation after generation, until the whole kind changes.
explain: After the dog came sheep and goats, then cattle and pigs, about 10,000 years ago; later horses, camels, llamas and chickens. They gave milk, wool, meat, eggs and muscle power, and let people settle in villages.
explain: Not every animal can be tamed. Zebras kick, bite and panic, and a working elephant in Asia, however gentle, was usually caught wild and trained, not tamed.
fact: Horses were first tamed about 5,000 to 6,000 years ago on the grasslands of what are now Ukraine, Russia and Kazakhstan.
fact: DNA shows that the horses we ride today come from a line first tamed near the Volga and Don rivers about 4,200 years ago.
fact: Przewalski's horse is a stocky wild horse from Mongolia that died out in the wild by the 1960s; zoo horses were released, and wild herds now live in Mongolia and even in the Chornobyl zone in Ukraine.
fact: All cattle come from the aurochs, a huge wild ox with long horns drawn in cave paintings; it was tamed about 10,000 years ago, and the last one died in a Polish forest in 1627.
fact: Dogs were tamed first, then sheep and goats, then cattle and pigs about 10,000 years ago, and later horses, camels and others.
fact: Zebras are nervous, kick and bite and are hard to catch, because they lived for a very long time alongside lions and human hunters; only a few people ever drove zebra carriages.
fact: Camels were tamed about 3,000 years ago and made desert trade possible; the hump stores fat, not water, and a thirsty camel can drink about 100 litres in about ten minutes.
fact: Chickens come from the red junglefowl, a wild bird of Asian jungles; they may first have been kept for show and fighting, later for eggs and meat, and there are now more chickens than any other bird on Earth.
fact: Silk comes from the cocoons of a moth caterpillar tamed in China about 5,000 years ago; the tame moths can no longer fly, and silk cloth was so precious and sold for so much along trade routes that China kept its secret for centuries.
fact: Llamas and alpacas were tamed in the Andes for wool and carrying; llamas may spit when annoyed, usually at other llamas, and some are used to guard sheep from foxes.
fact: Horses carried soldiers for thousands of years, millions died in the First World War, and a memorial in London remembers animals in war, including dogs and pigeons that carried messages.
fact: Elephants have worked with people for thousands of years but were never truly tamed; most were caught wild, as they breed slowly in captivity.
fact: Taming means changing a whole kind of animal over many generations, not training one animal.
exercise: Where were horses first tamed? | On the grasslands of what are now Ukraine, Russia and Kazakhstan | In the steamy jungles of Brazil, along the Amazon river | On the ice of Greenland | On the islands of Japan, in the far east of Asia
exercise: According to DNA, where did the line of today's riding horses begin? | Near the Volga and Don rivers about 4,200 years ago | In Egypt, beside the pyramids, about 100 years ago | In the green hills of England about 1,000 years ago | In Mongolia last century
exercise: What is special about Przewalski's horse? | It is a wild horse that died out in the wild and was brought back from zoos | It is the fastest racehorse ever, winner of every big race in the world | It is a horse with stripes | It is a tiny pony from the hills of Wales
exercise: Where else, besides Mongolia, do Przewalski's horses now live wild? | In the Chornobyl zone in Ukraine | In the middle of the Sahara desert | In the Amazon rainforest of Brazil | On the frozen islands of Antarctica
exercise: Which wild animal do all cows come from? | The aurochs | The bison | The buffalo | The yak
exercise: When and where did the last aurochs die? | In a Polish forest in 1627 | In Egypt 5,000 years ago | In a zoo in 1990 | They are still alive in Spain
exercise: Which animals did people tame first? | Dogs, then sheep and goats | Horses, then chickens and ducks | Cats, then camels and horses | Llamas, then elephants and tigers
exercise: Why were zebras never tamed? | They are nervous, kick and bite, and are hard to catch | They are far too small and weak for anyone to ride | They live only in the sea | Nobody ever tried, because people liked their stripes too much
exercise: What is in a camel's hump? | Fat | Water | Milk | Air
exercise: Which wild bird do chickens come from? | The red junglefowl of Asia | The wild turkey of North America | The pheasant of the European woods | The ostrich of the African plains
exercise: Which bird is the most common on Earth? | The chicken | The pigeon | The sparrow | The penguin
exercise: Where does silk come from? | The cocoons of a moth caterpillar | The webs of big jungle spiders | The wool of sheep | The soft inner bark of a mulberry tree
exercise: What happened to tame silk moths over thousands of years? | They can no longer fly | They grew as big as birds | They started making honey | They became wild again in the forests
exercise: Where were llamas and alpacas tamed? | In the Andes of South America | In the snowy Alps of Switzerland | In the dry outback of Australia | In the frozen forests of Siberia
exercise: Who does an annoyed llama usually spit at? | Other llamas | Every child it meets | Only its owner | Nobody, llamas never spit
exercise: Which animals carried messages in the First World War? | Dogs and pigeons | Cats and mice | Camels and llamas | Owls and bats
exercise: Why are working elephants not truly tamed? | Most were caught wild, as they breed slowly in captivity | They are too big and too clever to tame | They live only in zoos and never work | They were tamed long ago and then all went wild again
exercise: What is the difference between taming and training? | Taming changes a whole kind over generations; training teaches one animal | They mean exactly the same | Training takes thousands of years, but taming takes a week | Taming is teaching tricks to one circus animal so it obeys
test: «camel's hump is a water tank, that's how it crosses the desert» What is right? | The hump stores fat; a camel drinks a lot when it finds water | Yes, the hump is full of water that sloshes about | The hump is full of air | The hump stores sand to keep the camel heavy in the wind
test: Count them: a camel caravan has 6 camels, each with 1 hump. How many humps and how many legs? | 6 humps and 24 legs | 12 humps and 24 legs | 6 humps and 12 legs | 24 humps and 6 legs
test: Count them: at a petting zoo 3 llamas and 4 chickens. How many legs? | 20 | 14 | 28 | 24
test: «circus elephant can do tricks so it's tame, like a cow» What is right? | It was trained, but elephants as a kind were never tamed | Yes, elephants are just as tame as cows on a farm | All elephants are born tame | Elephants were tamed long before dogs, in the Stone Age
test: «why can't we just ride zebras, they're horses in pyjamas» What is the problem? | Zebras panic, kick and bite and have never been tamed | Zebras are too slow | Zebra stripes are sharp and hurt to sit on for long | It is against the law in every country of the world
test: What would happen if a farmer for 50 generations chose only the calmest junglefowl to breed? | The birds would become tamer, like chickens | They would turn into ducks | Nothing would change at all, because birds never change | They would slowly grow teeth and fur
test: «llama spat at me, they hate people» What is closer to the truth? | Llamas spit when annoyed, mostly at other llamas | Llamas spit at every single person on sight | Llamas never spit | They spit only at sheep, which they hate
test: In a cartoon a rooster is the boss who lays all the eggs. What is real? | Hens lay the eggs; roosters lay none | Roosters lay the biggest eggs in the coop | Both hens and roosters lay eggs every day | Only chicks lay eggs, before they grow up
test: «aurochs, never heard of it, made up» What is right? | It was a real wild ox, drawn in cave paintings, the ancestor of all cattle | It is a dinosaur | It is a cartoon dragon that breathes fire in old films | It is an alien from a video game, not a real animal at all
test: Why could a farmer with tame cows and goats stay in one village instead of wandering? | Animals gave milk, meat and wool close to home | Wild animals all left the area | Villages had no animals | Cows carried the whole village
test: Why did China guard the secret of silk so carefully? | Silk was precious and sold for a lot along trade routes | Silk was poisonous | Silk moths were dangerous and stung anyone who came near | Nobody wanted silk, so they kept it hidden from shame
exam: Identify the wild progenitor of all domestic cattle. | The aurochs, a large wild ox which became extinct in 1627 | The American bison, which still roams the plains in herds | The water buffalo of Asia | The mountain yak of the Himalaya and the high plateau of Tibet
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the contents of the camel's hump. | It contains a reserve of fat rather than of water | It contains a reserve of water | It contains a reserve of milk | It contains air for buoyancy
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the distinction between domestication and training? | Domestication alters a species over many generations, whereas training instructs an individual animal | Domestication and training are identical processes | Training alters a species over many generations, whereas domestication instructs a single animal within its lifetime | Domestication pertains exclusively to circus animals
exam: Ascertain the wild species from which the domestic chicken is descended. | The red junglefowl of the Asian jungles | The wild turkey of the forests of North America | The common pheasant of the woods of Europe | The ostrich of the African savannah and desert
